%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: a1.1 Moving in, staying in, greeting and inviting
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Four situated activities underpin our concept: moving into the care home (SA-01), waiting in one's bedroom for a reason to come out (SA-02), greeting someone new (SA-03), and inviting a fellow resident to spend time together (SA-04). A newcomer arrives disoriented, with little say in the transition, surrounded by strangers. Other residents stay in their bedrooms for months at a time. Shyness, forgotten names, and difficulty finding a topic stall most first conversations, and fear of rejection or intrusion keeps residents from inviting peers to join them. Care staff broker most introductions when their workload permits, relying on whatever personal history they happen to know on shift.

= Situated Activity - Moving into the care home [SA-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-01
|Name|Moving into the care home
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.

* Sury et al. (2013): moving into residential care is a period of loss and disorientation for people with dementia.
* Lai & Arthur (2003): wandering and exit-seeking in people with dementia.
* Victor (2012): loneliness is widespread in residential care homes.
)))
|Goal|Settle into the home, learn the layout and daily rhythm, recognise a few familiar faces, and regain a sense of personal control.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - moves into the home
* ST-02: Fellow resident - may welcome the newcomer
* ST-03: Relative - arranges the move and shares biographical history
* ST-04: Care worker - receives the newcomer and shows them around
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - records the life story, assigns dining seats, and introduces residents
* ST-06: Medical and therapy staff - assess cognitive and mobility needs at admission
)))
|Trigger|Living at home becomes unsustainable after a fall, a hospital admission, or caregiver exhaustion, and a room in the care home opens up.
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Pack belongings and say goodbye to home
1. Arrive at the care home and unpack
1. Learn the way between the bedroom, bathroom, dining room, and communal lounge
1. Learn the daily schedule and house routines
1. Meet care staff and fellow residents (SA-03)
1. Share biographical history and personal preferences
1. Find a regular place at table and a comfortable daily rhythm
)))
|Context|Admissions are often unplanned and decided by relatives or clinicians, leaving the resident little say over timing or room choice. The newcomer arrives knowing nobody. Staff rotate across shifts, and many fellow residents live with more advanced dementia and speak little. Corridors and doors look alike, exits are keypad-coded, and the unfamiliar layout compounds confusion. Meal times and house routines are fixed by the institution.
|Frequency and duration|Once per admission; settling in takes weeks to months.
|Tools and artifacts|Bedroom, name plate on the door, intake form, life-story book, care plan, house rules, weekly activity programme, coded doors.
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Bringing familiar furniture, ornaments, and family photographs from home
* Visiting the care home before admission day
* Recording a life story together with family members
* Assigning a primary care worker as a consistent point of contact
* Frequent family visits during the first days
* Staff-led introductions to table neighbours (SA-03)
* Maintaining familiar habits, such as reading the morning paper or having coffee at a set hour
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* The resident does not understand why they are in the home, or believes the stay is only for a few days
* The resident asks to go home and searches for an exit
* The resident cannot find their bedroom, the bathroom, or the dining room
* Grief over the house, spouse, pets, and neighbours left behind
* Resistance to care routines and resentment toward family or staff
* Having nobody to talk to during the day (PS-01)
* Meeting too many unfamiliar faces at once to remember any of them (PS-01)
* Being seated at meals among residents who rarely speak
* Retreating to the bedroom and skipping communal meals or activities (SA-02)
* Biographical history is never collected, or stays in a file that floor staff never read
* Family visits gradually become less frequent
* Social withdrawal goes unnoticed amid routine physical care
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-02 to ST-06); precedes → SA-02, SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-01.

= Situated Activity - Waiting for a reason to leave the room [SA-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-02
|Name|Waiting for a reason to leave the room
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.

* Jansen et al. (2017): nursing-home residents spend large portions of the waking day isolated in their private bedrooms.
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is widespread.
* Clare (2003): people aware of cognitive decline often withdraw socially to protect themselves from embarrassment.
)))
|Goal|Find a worthwhile, comfortable reason to leave the bedroom and spend time around others.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - stays in the bedroom and decides whether to come out
* ST-03: Relative - visits occasionally
* ST-04: Care worker - checks in briefly and brings care or meals
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - invites the resident to communal activities
)))
|Trigger|An empty schedule and no particular person to meet in the common areas, hardening into a daily habit over weeks or months.
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Wake, wash, and dress
1. Pass the hours watching television, looking out the window, or dozing
1. Listen for a knock at the door, a meal delivery, or a visitor
1. Weigh an invitation from staff to join the lounge or an activity
1. Step out to the common areas, or remain in the room
)))
|Context|The resident has lived in the home for months or years. Their private bedroom feels safe, quiet, and within their control, whereas the communal lounge is noisy and full of strangers. Staff check in briefly between care tasks. Earlier acquaintances in the home may have passed away, moved unit, or faded from memory.
|Frequency and duration|Daily, often spanning most waking hours.
|Tools and artifacts|Bedroom, armchair, television, window, call bell, meal tray, printed activity programme.
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Serving meals in the communal dining room
* Staff repeating invitations gently rather than asking only once
* Offering a familiar group activity, such as music or bingo
* Giving the resident a small, valued role, such as folding napkins or tending indoor plants
* Visits and outings with family members
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* No compelling reason arises, and the entire day passes alone in the bedroom
* Staff invitations focus on scheduled group events rather than meeting a compatible peer
* The resident declines repeated invitations until staff stop asking (PS-02)
* Daytime napping disrupts nighttime sleep
* Mood, confidence, and physical mobility decline gradually without triggering an alert (PS-02)
* The resident forgets the names of the few peers they once knew
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-03 to ST-05); may follow → SA-01; precedes → SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-02.

= Situated Activity - Greeting someone new [SA-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-03
|Name|Greeting someone new
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.

* Orange et al. (1996): conversational breakdowns become more frequent in Alzheimer's disease, and repair succeeds less often.
* Clare (2003): awareness of cognitive slips leads residents to guard against losing face.
)))
|Goal|Exchange names and a brief, pleasant conversation that leaves both residents willing to speak again without embarrassment.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - greets or responds to a peer
* ST-02: Fellow resident - greets or responds to ST-01
* ST-03: Relative - occasionally introduces residents during a visit
* ST-04: Care worker - introduces residents in passing
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - arranges seating and introduces compatible peers
)))
|Trigger|Encountering a newcomer (SA-01), sitting beside a new neighbour at meals, passing a peer in the lounge, or being introduced by staff.
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Notice the other resident and decide to speak
1. Offer a greeting
1. Exchange names
1. Mention a personal detail, such as one's hometown, former trade, or family
1. Ask a question, listen, and reply
1. Discover a shared topic or memory
1. Part on warm terms ("See you at dinner")
1. Recognise the person next time and recall their name
)))
|Context|Seating in the dining room and lounge is largely arranged by staff. Short-term memory loss erodes names, faces, and even one's own recent stories, while hearing or vision loss makes cues easy to miss. Making small talk with a stranger takes effort, and an awkward silence feels like a personal failure—especially in shared rooms where others can overhear.
|Frequency and duration|Daily; lasting from a few seconds to several minutes.
|Tools and artifacts|Communal lounge, dining tables, seating plan, door name plates, life-story books, photographs.
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Staff introduce and re-introduce residents by name, adding one shared detail to spark talk
* Staff connect residents who come from the same region, trade, or congregation
* Seating a newcomer beside an outgoing resident at meals
* Door name plates or table place cards
* Relying on immediate, low-memory topics such as the weather, the meal, or the view outside
* Using habitual courtesies ("Good morning", a nod, or a handshake)
* Looking through a photo album or life-story book together
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Both residents feel too shy to speak first
* Greeting a familiar neighbour as a stranger, or mistaking a stranger for someone else
* Forgetting the other person's name and feeling too embarrassed to ask again
* Avoiding a friendly acquaintance specifically to hide having forgotten their name (PS-03)
* Losing the thread mid-sentence and falling silent
* Running out of things to say until one person walks away
* Repeating the same question within minutes, which the other resident notices
* Mishearing a remark in a noisy room and merely nodding along
* Striking a tone that feels overly familiar or stiffly formal
* A quiet greeting goes unheard and is mistaken for a deliberate snub
* Conversation dies as soon as the staff member who introduced them walks away
* Being ignored when trying to join an existing group conversation
* Two residents who share a hometown, trade, or interest sit yards apart for months without ever finding out
* Staff mention a private detail about a resident without asking permission first
* Introductions depend on which caregiver is on shift rather than on residents' compatibility or wishes
* A flurry of first-day introductions is never followed up (PS-01)
* A warm first conversation never leads to a second meeting (PS-03)
* Avoiding a peer after an awkward or halting exchange
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-03 to ST-05); preceded-by → SA-01, SA-02; precedes → SA-04; illustrated-by → PS-03, PS-01 (in part).

= Situated Activity - Inviting someone to spend time together [SA-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-04
|Name|Inviting someone to spend time together
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.

* de Medeiros et al. (2012): residents with dementia in long-term care form and maintain meaningful friendships.
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common.
* Woods et al. (2018): structured reminiscence supports communication, mood, and quality of life.
* Astell et al. (2010): shared touchscreen media (photographs, music, film clips) scaffolds conversation in dementia.
* Särkämö et al. (2014): singing and listening to familiar music improve mood and episodic memory.
* Gonzalez & Kirkevold (2014): garden and horticultural activities increase social engagement and reduce agitation.
* Camp & Skrajner (2004): residents with milder dementia can lead structured small-group games for peers.
* Spector et al. (2003): themed cognitive stimulation activities improve communication and quality of life.
)))
|Goal|Spend enjoyable time with a fellow resident over a shared activity, while leaving both people free to decline or finish without losing face.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - extends or considers an invitation
* ST-02: Fellow resident - accepts or declines
* ST-03: Relative - occasionally includes a fellow resident during a visit
* ST-04: Care worker - suggests a shared activity, brings coffee or materials, or helps residents move to the lounge
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - pairs compatible residents and provides materials or icebreakers
* ST-06: Medical and therapy staff - note any clinical or mobility restrictions in the care plan
)))
|Trigger|Feeling lonely, noticing a peer sitting alone, discovering a shared interest during an introduction (SA-03), or receiving a gentle suggestion from staff.
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Choose a fellow resident to spend time with
1. Find an approachable moment to speak
1. Gauge whether the other person seems receptive
1. Suggest a shared activity and a time
1. Accept or decline the invitation
1. Remember the plan, meet in the common area, and begin
1. Bring the activity to a natural close, with the option to meet again
)))
|Context|(((
Invitations in a care home usually happen in communal rooms where others can overhear, making a direct refusal feel rude and an acceptance feel pressured. Having a concrete activity or topic in front of both residents eases the burden of continuous conversation and makes pauses comfortable. Many shared activities benefit from light staff support to set out materials or bring two residents to the same table.

Ordinary activities that work well for two residents include:

* Sharing coffee or tea, playing cards or board games, working on a jigsaw or crossword, reading the newspaper, or helping lay tables
* Reminiscing over old photographs, music, or objects from a familiar era (Woods et al., 2018), including on a shared touchscreen (Astell et al., 2010)
* Listening to or singing familiar songs from their youth (Särkämö et al., 2014)
* Tending indoor plants, potting herbs in a raised bed, or taking a stroll in the garden (Gonzalez & Kirkevold, 2014)
* Light word games, trivia, or sorting activities (Spector et al., 2003; Camp & Skrajner, 2004)
)))
|Frequency and duration|Uncommon without facilitation; typically 15 minutes to an hour when it occurs.
|Tools and artifacts|Communal lounge, dining tables, coffee and tea service, cards, board games, jigsaws, photo albums, nostalgic music or objects, touchscreen tablet, newspapers, garden patio and raised beds.
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Staff suggest a simple activity for two residents, such as having coffee together or looking at photos
* Seating the same pair near each other at meals or group events so familiarity grows
* Using a scheduled group activity as a low-pressure setting to sit side by side
* A visiting relative draws a neighbour into the conversation
* One resident joins another who is already occupied with a puzzle, music, or plants
* Staff ask two residents to help with a small shared chore, such as folding linen or watering plants
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* The initial greeting never happened or stalled awkwardly (SA-03)
* Neither resident asks, out of shyness or fear of being turned down
* Hearing loss or a flat facial expression is misread as disinterest
* A resident agrees only to be polite because the request was made face to face in front of others (PS-04)
* Neither person knows how to wind down the meeting, so it drags on until one feels trapped and tired (PS-04)
* An activity proves too demanding for one resident, leaving them feeling exposed
* A friendly invitation is misconstrued as romantic interest, causing avoidance
* Both residents forget the agreed time before the meeting happens
* No staff member is free to help bring the two residents together or set out materials
* In a group of three, one resident is inadvertently left out of the talk
* After a single refusal, the resident never risks asking anyone again
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-03 to ST-06); preceded-by → SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-04.

